<h2>Section 5: the scripted first response</h2>
<p>Section 5 applies to every police officer, Protection Officer, service provider or Magistrate who receives a complaint of domestic violence or is otherwise present at the place of an incident or when the incident is reported. The duty is informational and immediate — the woman must be told of:</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>her right to apply for one or more reliefs under the Act — protection, residence, monetary relief, custody and compensation orders;</li>
    <li>the availability of the services of registered service providers and of the Protection Officer;</li>
    <li>her right to free legal services under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987; and</li>
    <li>her right to file a complaint under the penal provision on cruelty by the husband or his relatives — now Section 85 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — where applicable.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>The proviso preserves the officer's ordinary duties: nothing in the section relieves the police of the obligation to proceed in accordance with law upon information of the commission of a cognizable offence. In other words, Section 5 adds duties; it subtracts none.</p>

<h2>Two tracks from one incident</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>The DV Act track (civil)</h4><p>Application under Section 12 before the Magistrate — in Delhi, the Mahila Courts — seeking protection, residence and monetary orders. No arrest, no FIR; interim and ex parte orders available under Section 23; breach of a protection order becomes an offence under Section 31.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">+</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>The criminal track (FIR)</h4><p>Where the facts disclose cognizable offences — cruelty (BNS 85), voluntarily causing hurt, criminal intimidation, dowry demands — an FIR under Section 173 BNSS sets investigation in motion. In Delhi, matrimonial complaints are ordinarily first processed through the CAW Cell\'s conciliation framework before registration, except in grave offences.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The tracks are independent. A woman may take both, either, or the civil track first and the criminal later; relief under the DV Act is not conditional on an FIR, and an FIR is not weakened by the absence of a DV application. What matters is that the choice be hers, made on correct information — which is precisely what Section 5 exists to guarantee.</p>

<h2>The supporting cast: Protection Officers and service providers</h2>
<p>The Protection Officer, appointed under Section 8, is the bridge between the woman and the Magistrate: preparing the Domestic Incident Report, assisting with the application, ensuring legal aid, arranging shelter homes under Section 6 and medical facilities under Section 7, and enforcing orders once passed. Registered service providers under Section 10 — typically NGOs — can record incidents, arrange medical examination and shelter, and their actions in good faith are protected. The police officer who receives the first complaint should connect the woman to this machinery, not substitute for it.</p>

<h2>What a well-handled first response looks like</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Hear and record</strong> — the complaint is received without demanding the husband's presence or counselling reconciliation as a precondition.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Inform</strong> — the Section 5 rights are explained: reliefs, Protection Officer, free legal aid, and the criminal complaint option.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Assess the offence</strong> — if a cognizable offence is disclosed, the FIR obligation under Section 173 BNSS is engaged; injuries are sent for medical examination.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Connect</strong> — details of the Protection Officer and service providers are shared; in urgent cases, shelter and medical access are arranged.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Document</strong> — a DD entry records the visit; the DIR, where prepared, goes to the Magistrate and the police station of jurisdiction.</div>
</div>

<h2>Remedies when the response fails</h2>
<p>Police inaction has defined escalation routes. For non-registration of an FIR in a cognizable matter: a written complaint to the Deputy Commissioner of Police under Section 173(4) BNSS, and thereafter an application under Section 175(3) BNSS before the Magistrate seeking a direction to register and investigate. For the civil reliefs, no police cooperation is needed at all — the Section 12 application lies directly before the Magistrate, who can call for a DIR and pass interim orders on the first date in a deserving case. A woman turned away from a police station has not lost her remedy; she has simply been pointed, wrongly, away from three parallel doors that all remain open.</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article describes statutory duties and remedies in general terms. It is not legal advice; individual situations call for advice on their own facts.</p>
</div>
